## 1. Underflow: one rule in the literal, the entry reader and the conversion

### 1.1 The rule

A **nonzero** number text whose nearest value at the target width is zero is a value the type
cannot hold. It is refused everywhere; it is never read as zero:

- literal: `SemanticDiag::LiteralNotRepresentable`, reason
  `too small to be represented (the nearest value is zero)`;
- entry argument: `RuntimeDiag::EntryArgumentInvalid`, reason
  `the value is too small for <T> (the nearest value is zero)`
  (`the <part> part is too small for <T>` for a complex part);
- conversion function: `ConversionOutOfRange` (the conversion has one "range" failure; see §7).

The boundary is half the smallest subnormal, i.e. the largest magnitude that **rounds to zero**:
`1.4e-45` is the smallest positive `f32` and is accepted; `1e-50` is refused. The same test is
used at each width (a `double` that is fine as a `double` can still underflow an `f32`).

### 1.2 What changed

| place | change |
|---|---|
| `include/camel/utils/number_text.h` | `NumberTextStatus` gained `Underflow`; `realValue` returns it when `from_chars` says `result_out_of_range` and a width-correct `strtof` / `strtod` gives a finite zero (and in the pre-20 libc++ fallback). `parseNumberToken` propagates it; `parseWholeFloat` folds it into `OutOfRange` because a conversion reports overflow and underflow with one diagnostic. |
| `src/compile/gct/builder.cpp`, `visitLiteral` (real branch) | `Underflow` commits the literal's own text and the "nearest value is zero" reason. |
| `src/compile/gct/builder.cpp`, `declaredValue` | when the exact value cannot become the declared type, `literal::Value::underflows(type)` selects the underflow reason instead of `out of range (...)`. |
| `src/compile/literal.cpp` / `include/camel/compile/literal.h` | `toFloating` no longer turns a tiny nonzero value into signed zero; a new `Value::underflows(Type *)` tests the exact rational against half the smallest subnormal of `f32`, `f64`, `c64` or `c128`. |
| `include/camel/core/data/primary.h` | `primaryFitsType` rejects a nonzero `double` that becomes `0.0f`; `literalRangeViolation` became `literalNotFitReason`, returning a whole reason clause (`out of range (...)` or the underflow clause), so the three GCT/GIR callers say the truth. |
| `src/compile/gir/builder_diagnostics.cpp` | `rejectLiteralValue` uses the underflow clause; `rejectLiteralData` uses the new reason function. |
| `src/execute/entry_args.cpp` | `loadFloat` and the complex part reader have an `Underflow` case with the truthful message. |
| `include/camel/standalone/entry.h` | `readNumber` maps `Underflow` to `ReadStatus::OutOfRange` (its public enum has no separate underflow). |
| `include/camel/utils/token.h` | pass parameters are read by `parseIntegerLiteral` / `parseDecimalReal`, which still had the old rules: `parseDecimalReal` turned a too-small nonzero value into zero, and `parseIntegerLiteral` took a leading-zero octal and did not know `0o`. A pass parameter is a source literal shape, so it now accepts `0o77` and refuses a nonzero underflow as well; the dead leading-zero-octal branch is gone. |

