## 2. `inf`, `-inf`, `nan`: the data-text supplement

The language already accepted these three forms in `parseWholeFloat` and `parseNumberToken`; the
work documents them and tests all three readers:

- an entry argument reads `inf`, `-inf` and `nan` for a float parameter;
- a conversion function reads them (`float('nan')`, `float('-inf')`);
- there is **no source literal**: source writes `math::inf` / `math::nan`
  (`stdlib/signatures/math.cml`), and `nan` / `inf` remain names.

`+inf` and `+nan` take the ordinary `+` sign (`parseNumberToken` allows it); a NaN carries no
printed sign. `INF`, `Infinity` and `+` inside the body stay refusals. This is the first of the
two supplements in language reference 2.5.2; the second is the leading-zero decimal of §6.

Tests added: `entry_args.float_inf`, `entry_args.float_negative_inf`, `entry_args.float_nan`; the
existing `entry_args.float_uppercase_inf_refused` still passes; `value.number_text` already
covered conversion `inf` / `-inf` / `nan`.

