## 7. Where the three readers still cannot share one rule

The review asks to say this plainly rather than paper over it. The shared rule is underflow:
**all three refuse**, and no reader returns zero. The remaining differences are deliberate and
are now documented:

1. **Conversion integer text is decimal only.** `parseWholeInteger` accepts an optional sign and
   decimal digits. The entry reader accepts a whole number token (`0x10`, `0b1010`, `0o77`), and a
   source literal accepts those too. So `int('0o77')` is `InvalidNumberText` while
   `--arg n=0o77` is 63. This is not introduced here (H15h's conversion grammar is unchanged) and
   is stated in 2.5.2 and in `number_text.h`; the leading-zero decimal `077` is accepted by both
   the entry reader and the conversion because it is plain decimal digits.
2. **A conversion has one range failure.** `ConversionOutOfRange` covers overflow and underflow;
   its message says "the value is outside the range of <T> (...)" even for underflow. The literal
   and the entry argument distinguish the two and say "the nearest value is zero". Changing the
   conversion enum/message would be a new user-visible diagnostic change outside this item; the
   review's requirement — refuse, do not return zero — is met, and §1.1 records the wording gap.
3. **`inf` / `nan` and a leading-zero decimal are data text only.** A source literal cannot be
   `nan` (it is a name) and cannot be `077` (lexical error); this is the point of the two
   supplements. A source program uses `math::nan` / `math::inf` and `0o77`.
4. **A pointed real is shared, but `077e2` is data text.** The entry reader accepts `077e2` as
   7700.0 (real-body grammar); source refuses it with the leading-zero lexical error. This follows
   from the same data-text supplement and is covered by the entry tests.
5. **A pass parameter is a fourth literal-shaped reader.** `parseIntegerLiteral` /
   `parseDecimalReal` in `include/camel/utils/token.h` had the old zero-underflow rule and the old
   leading-zero octal. It now refuses a nonzero underflow and accepts `0o77` as well (§1.2); this
   was not one of the review's "three readers", but leaving it behind would have been the same
   defect under another name.

